# ############################################################################
# DO NOT PUT REAL VALUES IN THIS FILE. IT IS TRACKED BY GIT.
#
# Copy it to `.env` in this folder first and fill that in. `.env` is gitignored;
# this is not. Somebody filled this one in directly, which is a reasonable thing
# to assume you can do with a file named like this, so the warning is here now
# rather than the assumption being called careless.
# ############################################################################

# The twelve secrets the new project needs, and nothing else.
#
# HOW TO USE
#   1. Copy this file to `.env` in the same folder.
#   2. Put each value after the `=`. No quotes, no spaces around the `=`.
#   3. Run:
#        npx supabase secrets set --env-file supabase/functions/.env --project-ref gpijpmepzowwhvgkriqu
#   4. Delete the `.env` afterwards. It is gitignored either way.
#
# The CLI reads the file itself, so the values do not have to be pasted
# anywhere else or shown on screen.
#
# WHY THESE TWELVE
#
# The old project has twenty-one secrets set, but seven of those Supabase
# injects on its own - SUPABASE_URL, SUPABASE_ANON_KEY, SUPABASE_SERVICE_ROLE_KEY,
# SUPABASE_DB_URL, SUPABASE_JWKS, SUPABASE_PUBLISHABLE_KEYS, SUPABASE_SECRET_KEYS.
# Setting those by hand would pin the new project to the old one's identity.
# Two more (PUSH_TRIGGER_SECRET, MEDIA_SWEEPER_SECRET) are further down, and
# come out of the new project's own vault rather than being invented.
#
# A dozen other names appear in the function code - CF_IMAGE_MODEL,
# NVIDIA_MODEL, SARVAM_TTS_SPEAKER and so on - and are deliberately absent:
# they were never set on the old project either, so each falls back to the
# default written beside it in code. Setting them here would change behaviour,
# not preserve it.
#
# WHERE EACH ONE COMES FROM
#
# None of these can be read back out of the old project. Supabase stores
# function secrets write-only: `secrets list` returns a SHA-256 of each value,
# not the value. They have to come from the service that issued them.

# --- Cloudflare (dash.cloudflare.com) -------------------------------------
# Account ID: on the right of any zone's overview page.
CF_ACCOUNT_ID=
# AI Gateway / Workers AI token, for image generation, TTS and vision.
CF_AI_TOKEN=
# TURN, used when a call cannot connect peer-to-peer. Calls still work without
# these for most people - see functions/turn-credentials/README.md.
CF_TURN_API_TOKEN=
CF_TURN_KEY_ID=
TURN_PROVIDER=

# --- Firebase (console.firebase.google.com) --------------------------------
# The service account JSON, base64 encoded. Push notifications do not work
# without it. Project settings -> Service accounts -> Generate new private key.
FCM_SERVICE_ACCOUNT_B64=

# --- LiveKit (cloud.livekit.io) --------------------------------------------
# Group calls. The URL is not secret but is set the same way.
LIVEKIT_API_KEY=
LIVEKIT_API_SECRET=
LIVEKIT_URL=

# --- Model providers --------------------------------------------------------
# NVIDIA NIM, which is what PINGO AI actually answers with.
NVIDIA_API_KEY=
# Sarvam, for speech in and out.
SARVAM_API_KEY=
# Hugging Face. Image generation only, and that path is idle - the account has
# no credits - so an empty value here changes nothing today.
HF_TOKEN=

# --- The two the database already knows ------------------------------------
#
# Do NOT invent these. They are shared secrets between the new database and
# these two functions, and the new project's vault already holds a freshly
# generated pair. Read them once in the new project's SQL editor:
#
#   select name, decrypted_secret from vault.decrypted_secrets
#    where name in ('push_trigger_secret', 'media_sweeper_secret');
#
# then paste each here. If the two sides disagree, push registers correctly and
# never arrives, and the media sweep stops without saying so.
PUSH_TRIGGER_SECRET=
MEDIA_SWEEPER_SECRET=
